<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 530 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave is a Celtic burial chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt period</a> near Hochdorf an der Enz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, dating from about 530 BCE. Excavated in 1978–1979, it held a man laid on a bronze couch 2.75 metres long with eight wheels, wearing a gold-plated torc and gold plaques on his shoes. In the chamber were also a four-wheeled wagon, drinking horns and dishes enough for nine people, and a cauldron imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a> that had held about 400 litres of mead.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave.jpg" alt="Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: marsupium photography · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hochdorf_Chieftain's_Grave_reconstruction.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The grave lies near Hochdorf an der Enz in the municipality of Eberdingen, within sight of the Hohenasperg, a fortified settlement and princely seat of the Hallstatt era surrounded by other elite burials such as the Grafenbühl grave and the Kleinaspergle. Its burial mound was originally 6 metres high and about 60 metres across, but centuries of erosion and farming had worn it down to about 1 metre, so that it could hardly be seen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>An amateur archaeologist found the grave in 1968. The excavation was carried out by the Baden-Württemberg Landesdenkmalamt under the archaeologist Jörg Biel, with the excavation technician Fritz Maurer. The dead man was about 50 years old and 180 cm tall. Besides his torc he wore a bracelet on his right arm and a hat of birch bark, and he was buried with a gold-plated dagger of bronze and iron, rich clothing, amber jewellery, a razor, a nail clipper, a comb, fishing hooks and arrows.</p><p>The cauldron at the foot of the couch is decorated with three lions around the brim and was altered after it came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a>. Hemp or cannabis was also found, the wagon was of wood plated with iron, and elaborate textiles lined the whole chamber. Krausse argued in 1999 that the burial may mark a man who was both chief and priest, a sacral king, and Gaspani held in 1998 that the diagonals of the chamber were aligned with the major lunar standstill, which recurs every 18.6 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the excavation the burial mound was rebuilt to its original height. The Keltenmuseum Hochdorf was built nearby and opened in 1991. While it was being built, the foundations of an ancient Celtic village were found, possibly the one to which the chieftain belonged, and these were incorporated into the museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Hochdorf</b>, near Stuttgart, a man buried about 530 BCE lay on a bronze couch almost three metres long, carried by eight small bronze women on wheels, with a four-wheeled wagon, drinking horns for nine, gold plaques on his shoes and a bronze cauldron made in the Greek…</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Some graves held a chariot or wagon, as at Vix, <b>Hochdorf</b>, Hohmichele and Býčí Skála, and wooden funerary carts, presumably used as hearses, were buried in the grandest.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…of southern Britain, with its rich barrow burials near Stonehenge from around 2000 BCE, the gold and bronze of Bronze Age Denmark and the princely graves of the Hallstatt culture, such as the <b>Hochdorf Chieftain’s Grave</b> of about 530 BCE, are widely read as the work of chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
